WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.175
Welcome to Part 2 of the deeper dive on
attribute-driven migration to Entra ID.

2
00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:10.260
This series goes below the technical deep
dive, down to the rule text, the commands,

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.737
the input files and the failing checks
an engineer actually works with.

4
00:00:14.737 --> 00:00:19.875
Part 1 covered Gates 0 to 3: reading
the organizational unit (OU) tree,

5
00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:26.226
fixing the schema and slots, proving the
attributes, and mapping them through sync.

6
00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:28.525
This part covers Gates 4 to 6.

7
00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:32.072
That's the dynamic group library
and administrative units,

8
00:00:32.072 --> 00:00:35.402
the Conditional Access
personas and device targeting,

9
00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:39.238
moving Group Policy for devices
and servers, and the PIV binding.

10
00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:41.507
Facts are as the workbook states them,

11
00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:45.760
design guidance is labeled, and
beta features carry their stage.

12
00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.185
Here's what this part delivers.

13
00:00:51.185 --> 00:00:55.760
Gate 4 is done when every governed rule
is written on codes with its guards,

14
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.110
tested and shadowed, and every
administrative unit mirrors its group.

15
00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.528
The validation kit's seven
dynamic group checks prove it,

16
00:01:03.528 --> 00:01:06.073
and the identity engineering lead owns it.

17
00:01:06.073 --> 00:01:10.921
Gate 5 is done when the personas come from
codes, every grant has a paired block,

18
00:01:10.921 --> 00:01:16.073
and the device, server and non-person
conventions are handed to their track guides.

19
00:01:16.073 --> 00:01:18.852
Gate 6 is done when
every pilot card holder

20
00:01:18.852 --> 00:01:21.711
is bound and high affinity
has been piloted.

21
00:01:21.711 --> 00:01:23.285
The CISO owns both.

22
00:01:23.285 --> 00:01:30.004
Each now has a kit check: VK-CA-01 for the
personas and VK-PIV-01 for the binding,

23
00:01:30.004 --> 00:01:34.773
with the policy export and
the sign-in logs alongside.

24
00:01:34.773 --> 00:01:37.997
Below the table are the
slots everything here reads.

25
00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:41.230
On users, organization code is slot 10,

26
00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:45.610
worker type 11, position
category 12, and division 6.

27
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:51.510
Devices have their own set: tier,
role and environment in 11 to 13.

28
00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.860
Gate 4 starts with the rule text,
because here a rule is an access grant.

29
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.898
Every governed group uses
one of two patterns.

30
00:02:02.898 --> 00:02:06.344
A node query selects people
by one organization code,

31
00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:10.260
and uses the in operator for a
roll-up instead of a chain of ors.

32
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.733
A functional query crosses the
organization with another coded attribute,

33
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:19.560
such as position category, to answer a
question that isn't about where someone sits.

34
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.385
Every access rule carries two guards.

35
00:02:22.385 --> 00:02:25.955
The account must be enabled, and
the user type must be Member,

36
00:02:25.955 --> 00:02:28.723
which keeps out disabled
accounts and guests.

37
00:02:28.723 --> 00:02:31.230
An agent's user account is a kind of user,

38
00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.285
so user rules include it
unless they exclude it.

39
00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:38.423
The books don't give the clause, so
prove yours with a named-user test.

40
00:02:38.423 --> 00:02:41.389
Rules read codes, never
department or job title,

41
00:02:41.389 --> 00:02:45.497
because a display string breaks
the next time someone retypes it.

42
00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:47.900
And privileged groups stay assigned.

43
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.862
Next, prove each rule
before it grants anything.

44
00:02:54.862 --> 00:02:59.838
The validation kit is read-only, and runs
with only the scopes its checks need.

45
00:02:59.838 --> 00:03:03.929
VK-DG-03 reads rule-tests.json:
a label, a member,

46
00:03:03.929 --> 00:03:09.788
the group or proposed rule, and
whether that person should match.

47
00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:14.975
It uses Graph's beta rule evaluation,
which works only with delegated sign-in,

48
00:03:14.975 --> 00:03:19.338
as at least a Groups Administrator;
an app-only run reports Error.

49
00:03:19.338 --> 00:03:22.700
A result that differs from what
you expected is a finding.

50
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.700
VK-DG-04 reads shadow-pairs.csv, the
group being replaced and its proposed

51
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:34.500
dynamic replacement, and lists everyone
the new group adds or removes.

52
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.578
By default no difference is allowed,

53
00:03:36.578 --> 00:03:41.150
so each one is explained in the record
before the rule grants production access.

54
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.362
A missing input means Not
Applicable, never Pass.

55
00:03:44.362 --> 00:03:48.998
And rollback is simple: a new group
grants nothing until it's assigned,

56
00:03:48.998 --> 00:03:53.410
so unassign it, keep the old group,
and revert the rule from Git.

57
00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.522
Two things change membership
without anyone touching a rule.

58
00:04:00.522 --> 00:04:01.798
The first is a date.

59
00:04:01.798 --> 00:04:06.026
The memberOf rule preview ends
on November 3, 2026, and groups,

60
00:04:06.026 --> 00:04:11.185
units and auto-assignment policies that
use it freeze in their last state.

61
00:04:11.185 --> 00:04:15.797
So Gate 4 uses no memberOf, and
flattens nested chains instead.

62
00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:21.772
VK-DG-06 lists any rule still using it,
and any group whose processing is paused,

63
00:04:21.772 --> 00:04:27.185
because a paused group stops updating
while every other check passes.

64
00:04:27.185 --> 00:04:28.685
The second is volume.

65
00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:34.045
VK-DG-05 compares each dynamic group's
member count with the snapshot from the

66
00:04:34.045 --> 00:04:39.322
last run, and flags a change of more than
ten percent and at least five members.

67
00:04:39.322 --> 00:04:43.984
The first run writes the baseline, so
take it now, before the mover proof.

68
00:04:43.984 --> 00:04:48.948
And plan for processing time: usually
a few hours, sometimes more than a day.

69
00:04:48.948 --> 00:04:54.390
For a bulk change, pause nonessential
groups for a day, then resume in phases.

70
00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.427
Administrative units replace
OU delegation, in three tiers.

71
00:05:02.427 --> 00:05:06.115
Tier 1 is read-only visibility,
and its reader roles

72
00:05:06.115 --> 00:05:09.715
can't be scoped to a unit,
so they sit at tenant scope.

73
00:05:09.715 --> 00:05:12.959
Tier 2 is the department
unit, whose rule lists the

74
00:05:12.959 --> 00:05:17.153
department's organization codes,
the same roll-up its group uses.

75
00:05:17.153 --> 00:05:23.240
Tier 3 is the division unit, created on
demand, reading the division code in slot 6.

76
00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:28.193
Scoped roles go to role-assignable groups
whose members are eligible through PIM,

77
00:05:28.193 --> 00:05:31.252
and each unit is created
before the groups it holds.

78
00:05:31.252 --> 00:05:34.078
The decision you can't
undo is restriction.

79
00:05:34.078 --> 00:05:38.978
Decide it before the unit exists, and
default to no, because restriction takes

80
00:05:38.978 --> 00:05:44.339
members out of PIM, entitlement management,
Lifecycle Workflows and access reviews.

81
00:05:44.339 --> 00:05:49.077
VK-DG-07 reads two small files:
one maps each unit to its group,

82
00:05:49.077 --> 00:05:54.007
and flags a unit whose rule doesn't
match; the other records why

83
00:05:54.007 --> 00:05:59.590
a unit is restricted, and flags
any restricted unit with no reason.

84
00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.497
Gate 5 retires Group Policy
inheritance for people,

85
00:06:06.497 --> 00:06:10.315
and the risk is policies that
look strict but restrict nothing.

86
00:06:10.315 --> 00:06:15.321
Personas come from codes: the internal
workforce from the worker type in slot 11,

87
00:06:15.321 --> 00:06:19.871
administrators from the account type
in slot 7, and the emergency-access

88
00:06:19.871 --> 00:06:23.815
group stays assigned and excluded
from every blocking policy.

89
00:06:23.815 --> 00:06:25.959
Target administrators by group,

90
00:06:25.959 --> 00:06:29.453
because role targeting misses
roles scoped to a unit.

91
00:06:29.453 --> 00:06:31.753
Then pair every grant with a block.

92
00:06:31.753 --> 00:06:36.455
A policy that grants Finance the payroll
app says nothing about anyone else,

93
00:06:36.455 --> 00:06:39.590
so a second policy blocks
everyone except Finance.

94
00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:43.091
And report empty slots
first: a policy on a dynamic

95
00:06:43.091 --> 00:06:47.190
group silently misses everyone
whose attribute is blank.

96
00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:52.095
VK-CA-01 checks three things on
every enabled and report-only policy:

97
00:06:52.095 --> 00:06:54.985
the emergency-access group is excluded,

98
00:06:54.985 --> 00:07:00.153
only governed groups are targeted,
and every grant has its paired block.

99
00:07:00.153 --> 00:07:04.531
It reads only who a policy targets,
so keep the ca-export as the record,

100
00:07:04.531 --> 00:07:07.737
and read it for blocks
narrower than their grants,

101
00:07:07.737 --> 00:07:10.910
with the list of users
whose slot 11 is empty.

102
00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:19.260
Gate 5 also sets the conventions the
device and server tracks build on.

103
00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.455
Device attributes come from device
provisioning, never from the user,

104
00:07:23.455 --> 00:07:26.710
and a device rule can never
read its owner's attributes.

105
00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.281
So the provisioning service writes tier,

106
00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:34.272
role and environment to the device's own
slots at registration, running app-only.

107
00:07:34.272 --> 00:07:38.598
Use a dynamic device group only where
the rule has to read those slots.

108
00:07:38.598 --> 00:07:42.857
Everywhere else, use an assignment
filter on Intune's own properties,

109
00:07:42.857 --> 00:07:45.248
like ownership or enrollment profile.

110
00:07:45.248 --> 00:07:48.097
A filter on a slot
simply doesn't evaluate.

111
00:07:48.097 --> 00:07:53.220
For anything time-sensitive, assign to a
user group or All devices with a filter,

112
00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.608
and never use a dynamic
device group as an exclusion,

113
00:07:56.608 --> 00:07:59.997
because late membership
lets the policy land first.

114
00:07:59.997 --> 00:08:05.233
VK-INT-01 flags managed
devices missing their slots,

115
00:08:05.233 --> 00:08:11.686
and VK-INT-04 flags any exclusion
built on a dynamic device group.

116
00:08:11.686 --> 00:08:14.648
For servers, Arc tags
reuse the same codes,

117
00:08:14.648 --> 00:08:18.320
and resource groups are
the delegation scope.

118
00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:24.920
Now the core of this
part: moving Group Policy.

119
00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.095
The rule is adopt, don't build.

120
00:08:27.095 --> 00:08:31.837
Microsoft already publishes the analysis
and the conversion, and the program

121
00:08:31.837 --> 00:08:36.433
adds only the decision record per setting,
the order of work, and the evidence.

122
00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:38.544
Phase 1 runs in five steps.

123
00:08:38.544 --> 00:08:43.812
First, Policy Analyzer and its command-line
companion find settings that several

124
00:08:43.812 --> 00:08:48.770
GPOs write with conflicting values, the
conflicts OU precedence used to hide.

125
00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:52.645
Second, export every GPO as XML.

126
00:08:52.645 --> 00:08:56.521
Third, import that into Intune's
Group Policy analytics,

127
00:08:56.521 --> 00:08:59.470
which shows each setting's MDM equivalent.

128
00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.683
Fourth, classify every setting, and capture
a gpresult baseline per device tier.

129
00:09:04.683 --> 00:09:06.395
Fifth, check it as data.

130
00:09:06.395 --> 00:09:13.007
VK-INT-07 reads the beta Graph migration
reports with a read-only permission.

131
00:09:13.007 --> 00:09:18.231
It flags any GPO still targeted in
AD whose readiness isn't complete,

132
00:09:18.231 --> 00:09:23.269
and, given your in-scope list, any
GPO that was never imported at all.

133
00:09:23.269 --> 00:09:26.413
And where an old GPO isn't
worth carrying forward,

134
00:09:26.413 --> 00:09:29.930
start from an Intune
security baseline instead.

135
00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.042
Phase 3 builds the policies
and assigns none of them.

136
00:09:37.042 --> 00:09:42.593
Group Policy analytics' Migrate step builds
a settings catalog policy from the GPOs.

137
00:09:42.593 --> 00:09:46.315
Select only the settings whose
decision record says required,

138
00:09:46.315 --> 00:09:49.106
review the values, and
leave assignments empty.

139
00:09:49.106 --> 00:09:50.343
Know where it stops.

140
00:09:50.343 --> 00:09:53.525
If two GPOs set one setting
to different values,

141
00:09:53.525 --> 00:09:55.892
it won't continue until you keep one.

142
00:09:55.892 --> 00:10:00.230
It's best effort, some settings fail
and are listed in Notifications, and

143
00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:05.304
AppLocker and firewall rules don't migrate
at all; build those in Endpoint security.

144
00:10:05.304 --> 00:10:10.235
Phase 4 pilots on about five percent of
the fleet, with the GPOs still linked,

145
00:10:10.235 --> 00:10:14.768
and compares each device's gpresult
with Intune's per-setting status.

146
00:10:14.768 --> 00:10:18.293
A conflict, an error or a
different value is a finding.

147
00:10:18.293 --> 00:10:22.243
For rollback, remember that the
pilot doesn't clean up after itself.

148
00:10:22.243 --> 00:10:26.674
Then, per population, back up
the GPO and disable the link,

149
00:10:26.674 --> 00:10:30.210
not the GPO, so one
command brings it back.

150
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.572
Servers have no Group Policy analytics.

151
00:10:36.572 --> 00:10:41.863
Microsoft publishes no tool that imports
a GPO into machine configuration,

152
00:10:41.863 --> 00:10:45.473
but it does publish a path,
and the program follows it.

153
00:10:45.473 --> 00:10:50.285
Step one, audit beside Group Policy:
let Group Policy keep enforcing while

154
00:10:50.285 --> 00:10:55.335
Azure Policy audits the same settings,
then move enforcement over gradually.

155
00:10:55.335 --> 00:10:56.498
Two cautions.

156
00:10:56.498 --> 00:10:59.365
Azure Policy settings
don't show up in gpresult,

157
00:10:59.365 --> 00:11:02.085
so the compliance reports
become the record.

158
00:11:02.085 --> 00:11:06.273
And per-user settings, like mapped
drives, stay with Group Policy.

159
00:11:06.273 --> 00:11:09.960
Step two, start from the built-in
baselines and definitions,

160
00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.197
which cover many settings
with no conversion at all.

161
00:11:13.197 --> 00:11:17.873
Step three, package what's left with
Microsoft's GuestConfiguration module;

162
00:11:17.873 --> 00:11:21.360
a package that applies settings
needs a recent Arc agent.

163
00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.276
Step four, treat a
converted GPO as a draft,

164
00:11:24.276 --> 00:11:29.422
because the conversion command is
community-supported, not Microsoft's.

165
00:11:29.422 --> 00:11:33.130
Test it in audit mode before
anything enforces it.

166
00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:42.035
Many estates already run Red Hat on AWS
with Ansible, and Arc can connect it:

167
00:11:42.035 --> 00:11:46.937
Red Hat is on the agent's list, and EC2
instances take the agent one by one,

168
00:11:46.937 --> 00:11:50.993
since the multicloud connector
isn't in Azure Government.

169
00:11:50.993 --> 00:11:53.467
The danger is two tools on one setting.

170
00:11:53.467 --> 00:11:56.998
Under auto-correct, Ansible
and machine configuration

171
00:11:56.998 --> 00:12:00.605
can flip a value back and forth
while both report success.

172
00:12:00.605 --> 00:12:04.480
So the Ansible guide sets five
rules, as design guidance.

173
00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.730
The owner is an attribute,
config_owner in the CMDB.

174
00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.772
Ansible writes only what it owns,

175
00:12:10.772 --> 00:12:14.855
enforced by its inventory group
and an assertion in the playbook.

176
00:12:14.855 --> 00:12:16.543
Onboarding isn't owning.

177
00:12:16.543 --> 00:12:18.642
An exception is a dated record.

178
00:12:18.642 --> 00:12:20.662
And patching has one owner too,

179
00:12:20.662 --> 00:12:24.379
so where Ansible patches,
Update Manager only assesses.

180
00:12:24.379 --> 00:12:27.355
Both owners are mirrored
to Arc tags at onboarding.

181
00:12:27.355 --> 00:12:30.875
VK-ARC-07 then catches
a server another tool

182
00:12:30.875 --> 00:12:35.450
owns that machine configuration
is also enforcing.

183
00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:42.275
One honest slide on what
the overview simplified.

184
00:12:42.275 --> 00:12:44.587
This framing is design guidance.

185
00:12:44.587 --> 00:12:47.501
First, many groups don't
follow the org chart.

186
00:12:47.501 --> 00:12:52.312
Only a group whose membership follows
attributes exactly becomes dynamic.

187
00:12:52.312 --> 00:12:57.290
Others stay synced, go cloud-only or
retire, privileged groups stay assigned,

188
00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:00.687
and what a rule can't decide
moves to access packages.

189
00:13:00.687 --> 00:13:02.025
Expect a lot of them.

190
00:13:02.025 --> 00:13:06.587
Second, Group Policy analytics tells
you what can move, not what should.

191
00:13:06.587 --> 00:13:09.758
It finds the landing zone;
it never decides whether a

192
00:13:09.758 --> 00:13:13.150
setting is still needed, so
retire before you translate.

193
00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:17.613
Third, running two tools side by
side is where breakage hides.

194
00:13:17.613 --> 00:13:21.644
GPOs beside Intune in the pilot,
Group Policy beside Azure

195
00:13:21.644 --> 00:13:26.012
Policy while auditing, Ansible
beside machine configuration.

196
00:13:26.012 --> 00:13:30.049
Each tests clean on its own,
and the overlap is what fails.

197
00:13:30.049 --> 00:13:32.741
So count the groups that won't go dynamic,

198
00:13:32.741 --> 00:13:35.870
and test every overlap,
not just each tool.

199
00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.720
Gate 6 is the PIV and CAC binding.

200
00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.601
Certificate sign-in is
attribute-driven too:

201
00:13:45.601 --> 00:13:49.961
Entra ID matches a field of the
certificate to a field of the account,

202
00:13:49.961 --> 00:13:54.632
so whoever writes that field decides
which certificate signs in as whom.

203
00:13:54.632 --> 00:13:59.294
The binding lives in
certificateUserIds, as prefixed values.

204
00:13:59.294 --> 00:14:01.958
Names and email addresses
are low affinity.

205
00:14:01.958 --> 00:14:04.412
The key identifier, the public key hash,

206
00:14:04.412 --> 00:14:09.244
and issuer with serial number are high
affinity, because they can't be reused.

207
00:14:09.244 --> 00:14:13.082
That also means a reissued or
renewed card changes the value.

208
00:14:13.082 --> 00:14:15.301
The limits: ten values per user,

209
00:14:15.301 --> 00:14:19.383
each unique in the tenant,
with case-sensitive prefixes.

210
00:14:19.383 --> 00:14:24.632
For cloud-only users, a Privileged
Authentication Administrator or Graph writes it.

211
00:14:24.632 --> 00:14:29.935
For synced users, only Entra Connect
can, from altSecurityIdentities in AD,

212
00:14:29.935 --> 00:14:33.357
and Microsoft doesn't
document Cloud Sync for it.

213
00:14:33.357 --> 00:14:36.850
Treat that AD write path
like an access slot.

214
00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:43.350
The rest of Gate 6 is a short sequence.

215
00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.274
Measure coverage for
every pilot card holder,

216
00:14:46.274 --> 00:14:49.813
and list the gaps by account,
never by certificate value.

217
00:14:49.813 --> 00:14:53.148
Name the writer: the card
management system, not HR,

218
00:14:53.148 --> 00:14:58.112
because a reissued card changes the
binding, while HR supplies the person.

219
00:14:58.112 --> 00:14:59.987
Decide the required affinity.

220
00:14:59.987 --> 00:15:04.329
The tenant default is low, and sign-in
is only as strong as the weakest

221
00:15:04.329 --> 00:15:08.438
binding allowed, so the program's
position is high in production.

222
00:15:08.438 --> 00:15:11.285
Pilot high affinity with
a rule per issuer before

223
00:15:11.285 --> 00:15:13.924
you change the tenant, or
people get locked out.

224
00:15:13.924 --> 00:15:17.613
Make the leaver remove the binding
as well as revoke the card.

225
00:15:17.613 --> 00:15:19.787
And decide who writes it after AD.

226
00:15:19.787 --> 00:15:24.299
The design is that the card system
writes it through Graph at issuance,

227
00:15:24.299 --> 00:15:27.799
but that's unverified, and it's
an open pilot in issue 130.

228
00:15:27.799 --> 00:15:34.019
VK-PIV-01 proves coverage: it lists
pilot card holders with no value,

229
00:15:34.019 --> 00:15:37.638
or only low-affinity values, by account.

230
00:15:37.638 --> 00:15:40.390
It can't tell whether a
value is a current card,

231
00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:44.400
so reconcile with the card system,
and check the sign-in logs.

232
00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:52.350
Four decisions close this part, each
with one owner from the project plan.

233
00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:57.975
Identity engineering decides restriction,
unit by unit, before each unit exists.

234
00:15:57.975 --> 00:16:02.850
The CISO decides the persona set,
and the required affinity for PIV.

235
00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:08.162
And the ICAM governance body decides who
writes the binding after AD; that one

236
00:16:08.162 --> 00:16:13.475
can wait until issuance moves off AD CS,
which gives the open pilot time to report.

237
00:16:13.475 --> 00:16:15.187
Watch the margins, too.

238
00:16:15.187 --> 00:16:18.813
The tightest here is just two
working days, between the pilot's

239
00:16:18.813 --> 00:16:22.724
shadow comparison and the program
assigning the pilot's policies.

240
00:16:22.724 --> 00:16:27.821
Part 3 takes Gates 7 to 9: proving one
mover across the tracks with no ticket,

241
00:16:27.821 --> 00:16:31.748
cutting over by attribute
query and retiring the OU tree,

242
00:16:31.748 --> 00:16:34.338
and then keeping the attributes true.

243
00:16:34.338 --> 00:16:35.160
Thank you.
